---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
const schema =`
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
"@context":"https://schema.org",
"@type":"WebApplication",
"name":"stlscale — STL Scale Converter",
"url":"https://stlaccess.com/",
"description":"Browser-based calculator that outputs resize percentages to scale STL files between tabletop gaming systems. Based on a 175cm human baseline with eye-level and top-of-head options.",
"applicationCategory":"UtilityApplication",
"operatingSystem":"Web",
"browserRequirements":"Requires JavaScript. No login required.",
"offers":{
"@type":"Offer",
"price":"0",
"priceCurrency":"USD"
},
"featureList":"Convert between 15+ gaming scales, Eye Level and Top of Head measurement options, Warhammer and D&D conversions, Browser-based with no file upload",
"creator":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://stlaccess.com/about"
}
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
"@context":"https://schema.org",
"@type":"WebSite",
"@id":"https://stlaccess.com/#website",
"url":"https://stlaccess.com/",
"name":"STLACCESS",
"description":"Browser-based STL scale converter for 3D printed miniatures. Free calculator for Warhammer, D&D, and tabletop gaming scales.",
"publisher":{"@id":"https://stlaccess.com/#person"},
"inLanguage":"en"
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
"@context":"https://schema.org",
"@type":"Person",
"@id":"https://stlaccess.com/#person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"alternateName":"stlscale",
"url":"https://stlaccess.com/",
"jobTitle":"3D Printing Hobbyist",
"knowsAbout":[
  "3D printing miniatures",
  "tabletop wargaming scales",
  "FDM printing",
  "Warhammer 40k scale",
  "D&D miniature scaling",
  "STL file conversion"
],
"sameAs":[
"https://github.com/kitsorix-official/stlaccess"
]
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
"@context":"https://schema.org",
"@type":"WebPage",
"@id":"https://stlaccess.com/#webpage",
"name":"STL Scale Converter for 3D Printed Miniatures",
"url":"https://stlaccess.com/",
"speakable":{
"@type":"SpeakableSpecification",
"cssSelector":[
"#hero-description",
"#faq details summary",
"#reference-intro"
]
},
"isPartOf":{
"@id":"https://stlaccess.com/#website"
}
}
</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
"@context":"https://schema.org",
"@type":"QAPage",
"mainEntity":[
{
"@type":"Question",
"name":"What scale is Warhammer 40k miniatures?",
"text":"What scale is Warhammer 40k miniatures?",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
},
"acceptedAnswer":{
"@type":"Answer",
"text":"Most people in the hobby treat Games Workshop miniatures as 32mm heroic scale, measured from eye level at roughly 160cm. To convert a 28mm STL to 32mm heroic, scale it to 114.3% in your slicer. Your mileage may vary depending on the sculpt.",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
}
}
},
{
"@type":"Question",
"name":"How do I convert 28mm to 32mm for D&D?",
"text":"How do I convert 28mm to 32mm for D&D?",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
},
"acceptedAnswer":{
"@type":"Answer",
"text":"Multiply your 28mm STL file by 114.3% to get what most players call heroic 32mm. This uses a 175cm human baseline and eye-level measurement, which is what I use on STLACCESS because it matches my prints. If your group uses something different, adjust accordingly.",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
}
}
},
{
"@type":"Question",
"name":"Does material shrinkage affect FDM miniatures?",
"text":"Does material shrinkage affect FDM miniatures?",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
},
"acceptedAnswer":{
"@type":"Answer",
"text":"PLA and PETG shrink about 0.2-0.5% during cooling. This is small enough that I do not add compensation. If you want maximum accuracy, print a test piece first and measure with calipers.",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
}
}
},
{
"@type":"Question",
"name":"What is the difference between 28mm and 32mm miniatures?",
"text":"What is the difference between 28mm and 32mm miniatures?",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
},
"acceptedAnswer":{
"@type":"Answer",
"text":"28mm true scale measures a 175cm human from eye level. 32mm heroic uses the same baseline but adds bigger hands, heads, and weapons for detail and durability. A 32mm miniature ends up roughly 14% taller than its 28mm equivalent. Games Workshop uses 32mm heroic for Warhammer 40k. A lot of D&D and Pathfinder miniatures are closer to 28mm true scale, though it varies by manufacturer.",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
}
}
},
{
"@type":"Question",
"name":"How do I scale an STL file in my slicer?",
"text":"How do I scale an STL file in my slicer?",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
},
"acceptedAnswer":{
"@type":"Answer",
"text":"Import the STL into your slicer (Chitubox, Lychee, PrusaSlicer, or Cura). Select the model, open the scale tool, and enter the percentage from stlscale. Lock the X, Y, and Z axes to uniform scaling. Apply the change and slice as normal.",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
}
}
},
{
"@type":"Question",
"name":"What does 1:56 scale mean in miniature gaming?",
"text":"What does 1:56 scale mean in miniature gaming?",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
},
"acceptedAnswer":{
"@type":"Answer",
"text":"1:56 is a ratio scale common in historical wargaming, including Bolt Action. It works out to roughly 28mm true scale if you use a 175cm human baseline. To convert 1:56 STLs to 32mm heroic, most people scale them to 114.3%.",
"author":{
"@type":"Person",
"name":"kitsorix",
"url":"https://github.com/kitsorix-official"
}
}
}
]
}
</script>`;
---
<Layout
title="STL Scale Converter | 28mm to 32mm, Warhammer & D&D | stlscale"
description="STL scale converter for 3D printed miniatures. Calculates resize percentages for 28mm to 32mm, 1:35 to 32mm, and other tabletop gaming scales. Runs in your browser. No file upload."
schema={schema}
>
<nav class="sr-only" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
  <span aria-current="page">Home</span>
</nav>

<!-- Hero Section -->
<section class="pt-20 pb-16 px-6 text-center max-w-4xl mx-auto">
<p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-[#ff6b35]/70 mb-6">
STLSCALE Engine v1.0 — Scaling for Miniature Printers
</p>
<h1 class="text-4xl md:text-7xl font-bold mb-6 font-heading tracking-tight leading-tight">
STL Scale Converter<br />
<span class="text-[#ff6b35] italic">for 3D Printed Miniatures</span>
</h1>
<p id="hero-description" class="text-lg md:text-xl text-slate-300 mb-4 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
<span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">stlscale</span> is a browser-based calculator I built because I got tired of doing the math in my head every time I resized an STL. Pick your source scale and target system. It returns the multiplier based on a <span class="text-white font-semibold">175cm human baseline</span> — the same one most of the hobby uses, though not everyone agrees on the details. No file upload. No login.
</p>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500">
Warhammer 40K, D&D, Star Wars Legion, Bolt Action, and a bunch of historical systems.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Engine App Container -->
<section id="engine" class="px-6 pb-16" aria-labelledby="engine-heading">
<h2 id="engine-heading" class="sr-only">Scale Calculator</h2>
<div class="max-w-xl mx-auto bg-slate-900/40 p-1 rounded-[2.5rem] border border-white/10 glow-box">
<div class="bg-[#0a0a0a] p-8 md:p-10 rounded-[2.2rem] space-y-8 relative">

<!-- Input: Source -->
<div class="space-y-3">
<label for="sourceScale" class="block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-500">
Source scale
</label>
<div class="relative">
<select id="sourceScale" class="w-full bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 text-white rounded-2xl px-5 py-4 focus-ring appearance-none cursor-pointer transition-all hover:border-slate-600">
<optgroup label="Ratio Scales">
<option value="1:100">1:100 (15mm Team Yankee)</option>
<option value="1:72">1:72 (Plastic Soldiers)</option>
<option value="1:56">1:56 (Bolt Action / 28mm)</option>
<option value="1:48">1:48 (Quarter Scale)</option>
<option value="1:35">1:35 (Military Model Kits)</option>
</optgroup>
<optgroup label="System Scales">
<option value="28mm" selected>28mm (True Scale)</option>
<option value="32mm">32mm (Heroic Scale)</option>
<option value="35mm">35mm (Star Wars Legion)</option>
</optgroup>
</select>
<div class="absolute right-5 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 pointer-events-none text-slate-500">
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-xs"></i>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- Input: Target -->
<div class="space-y-3">
<label for="targetScale" class="block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-500">
Target scale
</label>
<div class="relative">
<select id="targetScale" class="w-full bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 text-white rounded-2xl px-5 py-4 focus-ring appearance-none cursor-pointer transition-all hover:border-slate-600">
<option value="28mm">D&D / Pathfinder (28mm)</option>
<option value="32mm" selected>Warhammer / 32mm Heroic</option>
<option value="35mm">Star Wars Legion (35mm)</option>
<option value="40mm">Shatterpoint (40mm)</option>
<option value="75mm">Display Miniatures (75mm)</option>
<option value="1:35">Historical Diorama (1:35)</option>
</select>
<div class="absolute right-5 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 pointer-events-none text-slate-500">
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-xs"></i>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- Input: Logic Toggle -->
<fieldset class="bg-black/40 p-2 rounded-2xl border border-white/5">
<legend class="sr-only">Measurement method</legend>
<div class="flex gap-2">
<label class="flex-1 text-center py-3 rounded-xl cursor-pointer transition-all font-semibold text-sm relative group">
<input type="radio" name="logic" value="eye" class="sr-only" checked />
<div class="bg-transparent border border-transparent py-2 rounded-lg group-has-[:checked]:bg-slate-800 group-has-[:checked]:border-white/10 group-has-[:checked]:text-[#ff6b35]">
Eye Level
</div>
</label>
<label class="flex-1 text-center py-3 rounded-xl cursor-pointer transition-all font-semibold text-sm relative group">
<input type="radio" name="logic" value="head" class="sr-only" />
<div class="bg-transparent border border-transparent py-2 rounded-lg group-has-[:checked]:bg-slate-800 group-has-[:checked]:border-white/10 group-has-[:checked]:text-[#ff6b35]">
Top of Head
</div>
</label>
</div>
</fieldset>

<!-- Input: Shrinkage Compensation -->
<div class="bg-black/40 p-4 rounded-2xl border border-white/5 space-y-4">
<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
<div class="space-y-1">
<h3 class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-400">
Material compensation
</h3>
<p class="text-[10px] text-slate-500">
Add a small percentage for cooling shrinkage
</p>
</div>
<label class="relative inline-flex items-center cursor-pointer">
<input type="checkbox" id="useShrinkage" class="sr-only peer" />
<div class="w-11 h-6 bg-slate-800 peer-focus:outline-none rounded-full peer peer-checked:after:translate-x-full rtl:peer-checked:after:-translate-x-full peer-checked:after:border-white after:content-[''] after:absolute after:top-[2px] after:start-[2px] after:bg-white after:border-gray-300 after:border after:rounded-full after:h-5 after:w-5 after:transition-all peer-checked:bg-[#ff6b35]"></div>
</label>
</div>
<div id="shrinkageInputContainer" class="hidden animate-in fade-in slide-in-from-top-2 duration-300">
<div class="relative">
<input type="number" id="shrinkageRate" value="1.5" step="0.1" min="0" max="10" class="w-full bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 text-white rounded-xl px-4 py-3 focus-ring transition-all hover:border-slate-600 text-sm font-bold" />
<div class="absolute right-4 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 text-slate-500 text-xs font-bold uppercase">
% Rate
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- Result Section -->
<div class="pt-4" role="region" aria-live="polite">
<div class="bg-[#ff6b35] rounded-[2rem] p-8 text-black text-center relative overflow-hidden">
<div class="absolute top-0 right-0 p-4 opacity-10" aria-hidden="true">
<i class="fa-solid fa-print text-6xl -rotate-12"></i>
</div>
<span class="text-[10px] font-black uppercase tracking-[0.2em] mb-2 block opacity-80">
Slicer scale
</span>
<div id="resultDisplay" class="text-6xl md:text-7xl font-black font-heading tracking-tighter leading-none">
</div>
<div class="mt-6 flex gap-3">
<button onclick="if(window.copyToClipboard) copyToClipboard()" id="copyBtn" class="flex-1 py-4 bg-black text-white rounded-xl font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-widest hover:bg-black/80 transition-all flex items-center justify-center gap-2 focus-ring">
<i class="fa-regular fa-copy"></i>
<span>Copy</span>
</button>
<button onclick="if(window.saveToLibrary) saveToLibrary()" id="saveBtn" class="flex-1 py-4 bg-white/20 border-2 border-black text-black rounded-xl font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-widest hover:bg-white/30 transition-all flex items-center justify-center gap-2 focus-ring">
<i class="fa-solid fa-folder-plus"></i>
<span>Save</span>
</button>
</div>
<div class="mt-4 flex items-center justify-center gap-2 text-[10px] text-black/60 font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
<i class="fa-solid fa-shield-halved"></i>
175cm baseline
<span class="mx-1">•</span>
<a href="/about" class="hover:underline">Methodology</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<!-- Library Preview -->
<div id="libraryPreview" class="hidden bg-slate-900/50 rounded-2xl p-4 border border-white/5">
<div class="flex justify-between items-center mb-3">
<span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-400">Recently saved</span>
<a href="/library" class="text-xs text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors">View all →</a>
</div>
<div id="recentScales" class="space-y-2">
<!-- Populated by JS -->
</div>
</div>

<p class="text-[11px] text-slate-500 text-center leading-relaxed px-4">
Conversion logic assumes a 175cm human baseline. Eye Level measures to 160cm. Top of Head accounts for hair and helmets at 175cm.
<br />
<strong class="text-slate-400">Tip:</strong> Always use <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">Uniform Scaling</span> (lock X, Y, and Z axes) to avoid distorted models.
</p>

</div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- How to Use -->
<section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-12" aria-labelledby="howto-heading">
<h2 id="howto-heading" class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold font-heading mb-8">
How to scale your <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">STL</span>
</h2>
<ol class="space-y-6">
<li class="flex gap-4 items-start">
<span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm border border-[#ff6b35]/20">1</span>
<div>
<p class="text-slate-200 font-semibold">Select your scales</p>
<p class="text-slate-400 text-sm">Pick source and target in the calculator above. Choose Eye Level or Top of Head.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="flex gap-4 items-start">
<span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm border border-[#ff6b35]/20">2</span>
<div>
<p class="text-slate-200 font-semibold">Copy the percentage</p>
<p class="text-slate-400 text-sm">Open your STL in <span class="text-white">Chitubox</span>, <span class="text-white">Lychee</span>, <span class="text-white">PrusaSlicer</span>, or <span class="text-white">Cura</span>.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li class="flex gap-4 items-start">
<span class="flex-shrink-0 w-10 h-10 rounded-full bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm border border-[#ff6b35]/20">3</span>
<div>
<p class="text-slate-200 font-semibold">Paste and lock axes</p>
<p class="text-slate-400 text-sm">Enter the percentage into the scale tool. Lock <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">X, Y, and Z</span> to uniform scaling. Slice and print.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</section>

<!-- Reference Section -->
<section id="reference" class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-white/5">
<div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row justify-between items-end gap-6 mb-12">
<div class="max-w-xl">
<h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">
Popular <span class="text-[#ff6b35] italic">scale conversions</span>
</h2>
<p id="reference-intro" class="text-slate-400 text-lg">
Percentages for the most common tabletop wargaming and historical modeling transitions. These are what I use and what the community has landed on, but if something looks off on your printer, <a href="/about" class="text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors">let me know</a>.
</p>
</div>
<a href="/reference" class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-widest border-b-2 border-[#ff6b35]/20 hover:border-[#ff6b35] pb-1 transition-all">
View full scale reference library
</a>
</div>
<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
<a href="/reference" class="group block bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 hover:border-[#ff6b35]/40 transition-all hover:bg-slate-900/50">
<span class="text-[10px] font-bold text-[#ff6b35] uppercase tracking-widest block mb-4">Most searched</span>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 group-hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">28mm to 32mm</h3>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">Warhammer 40K and D&D heroic scale</p>
<div class="text-4xl font-bold font-heading text-[#ff6b35]">114.3%</div>
</a>
<a href="/reference/1-35-to-32mm" class="group block bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 hover:border-[#ff6b35]/40 transition-all hover:bg-slate-900/50">
<span class="text-[10px] font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest block mb-4">Historical</span>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 group-hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">1:35 to 32mm</h3>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">Military kits to heroic gaming scale</p>
      <div class="text-4xl font-bold font-heading text-[#ff6b35]">64.0%</div>
</a>
<a href="/reference/1-48-to-28mm" class="group block bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 hover:border-[#ff6b35]/40 transition-all hover:bg-slate-900/50">
<span class="text-[10px] font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest block mb-4">Quarter scale</span>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 group-hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">1:48 to 28mm</h3>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">Aircraft and vehicle models to true 28mm</p>
<div class="text-4xl font-bold font-heading text-[#ff6b35]">76.8%</div>
</a>
<a href="/reference/1-56-to-28mm" class="group block bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 hover:border-[#ff6b35]/40 transition-all hover:bg-slate-900/50">
<span class="text-[10px] font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest block mb-4">Bolt Action</span>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 group-hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">1:56 to 28mm</h3>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">WWII wargaming to true 28mm</p>
<div class="text-4xl font-bold font-heading text-[#ff6b35]">89.6%</div>
</a>
<a href="/reference" class="group block bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 hover:border-[#ff6b35]/40 transition-all hover:bg-slate-900/50">
<span class="text-[10px] font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest block mb-4">Display</span>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 group-hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">32mm to 75mm</h3>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">Gaming minis to display painting scale</p>
<div class="text-4xl font-bold font-heading text-[#ff6b35]">234.4%</div>
</a>
<a href="/reference" class="group block bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 hover:border-[#ff6b35]/40 transition-all hover:bg-slate-900/50">
<span class="text-[10px] font-bold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-widest block mb-4">Star Wars</span>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2 group-hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">28mm to 35mm</h3>
<p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mb-6">True scale to Star Wars Legion</p>
<div class="text-4xl font-bold font-heading text-[#ff6b35]">125.0%</div>
</a>
</div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ Section -->
<section id="faq" class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-white/5" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
<div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row justify-between items-end gap-6 mb-12">
<div class="max-w-xl">
<h2 id="faq-heading" class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold font-heading mb-4">
Scale conversion <span class="text-[#ff6b35] italic">questions</span>
</h2>
<p class="text-slate-400 text-lg">
Direct answers to the scaling questions I get asked most. If yours is not here, <a href="/about" class="text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors">send it in</a>.
</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="space-y-4">
<details class="bg-slate-900/30 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden group open:bg-slate-900/50 transition-all">
<summary class="p-8 cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">
<span>What scale is <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">Warhammer 40k</span> miniatures?</span>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300"></i>
</summary>
<div class="px-8 pb-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
<p>
Most people in the hobby treat Games Workshop miniatures as <span class="text-white font-semibold">32mm heroic scale</span>, measured from eye level at roughly 160cm. To convert from true 28mm, scale your STL to <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold text-lg">114.3%</span> in your slicer. Your mileage may vary depending on the sculpt.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="bg-slate-900/30 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden group open:bg-slate-900/50 transition-all">
<summary class="p-8 cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">
<span>How do I convert <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">28mm to 32mm</span> for D&D?</span>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300"></i>
</summary>
<div class="px-8 pb-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
<p>
Multiply your 28mm STL file by <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold text-lg">114.3%</span> to get what most players call heroic 32mm scale. This accounts for the <span class="text-white font-semibold">175cm human baseline</span> and <a href="/about" class="text-white border-b border-white/20 hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">eye-level measurement</a> I use on STLACCESS. If your group uses something different, adjust accordingly.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="bg-slate-900/30 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden group open:bg-slate-900/50 transition-all">
<summary class="p-8 cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">
<span>Does <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">material shrinkage</span> affect FDM miniatures?</span>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300"></i>
</summary>
<div class="px-8 pb-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
<p>
PLA and PETG shrink about <span class="text-white font-semibold">0.2-0.5%</span> during cooling. This is small enough that I do not add compensation. If you want maximum accuracy, print a test piece first and measure with calipers.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="bg-slate-900/30 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden group open:bg-slate-900/50 transition-all">
<summary class="p-8 cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">
<span>What is the difference between <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">28mm and 32mm</span> miniatures?</span>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300"></i>
</summary>
<div class="px-8 pb-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
<p>
<span class="text-white font-semibold">28mm true scale</span> measures a 175cm human from eye level. <span class="text-white font-semibold">32mm heroic scale</span> uses the same baseline but adds exaggerated proportions for detail and durability. A 32mm miniature ends up roughly <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold">14% taller</span> than its 28mm equivalent.
</p>
<p class="mt-4">
Games Workshop uses 32mm heroic for Warhammer 40k. A lot of D&D and Pathfinder miniatures are closer to 28mm true scale, though it varies by manufacturer.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="bg-slate-900/30 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden group open:bg-slate-900/50 transition-all">
<summary class="p-8 cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">
<span>How do I scale an <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">STL file</span> in my slicer?</span>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300"></i>
</summary>
<div class="px-8 pb-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
<p>
Import the STL into your slicer (<span class="text-white">Chitubox</span>, <span class="text-white">Lychee</span>, <span class="text-white">PrusaSlicer</span>, or <span class="text-white">Cura</span>). Select the model, open the scale tool, and enter the percentage from stlscale. Lock the <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">X, Y, and Z</span> axes to uniform scaling. Apply the change and slice as normal.
</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="bg-slate-900/30 rounded-3xl border border-white/5 overflow-hidden group open:bg-slate-900/50 transition-all">
<summary class="p-8 cursor-pointer list-none flex justify-between items-center font-bold text-lg hover:text-[#ff6b35] transition-colors">
<span>What does <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">1:56 scale</span> mean in miniature gaming?</span>
<i class="fa-solid fa-chevron-down text-slate-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300"></i>
</summary>
<div class="px-8 pb-8 text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
<p>
1:56 is a ratio scale common in historical wargaming, including <span class="text-white font-semibold">Bolt Action</span>. It works out to roughly 28mm true scale when you use a 175cm human baseline. To convert 1:56 STLs to 32mm heroic, most people scale them to <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-bold text-lg">114.3%</span>.
</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
</section>

<!-- About / EEAT Section -->
<section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 border-t border-white/5" aria-label="About STLACCESS">
<h2 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold font-heading mb-6">
Why I built <span class="text-[#ff6b35] italic">stlscale</span>
</h2>
<p class="text-lg text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
I got tired of calculating the same percentages manually in <span class="text-white font-semibold">Chitubox</span> every time I resized an STL for my Warhammer or D&D prints. I would forget which baseline I used last time and end up with minis that looked wrong next to my existing army. Sometimes too tall. Sometimes weirdly squat. It annoyed me enough to build this.
</p>
<p class="text-lg text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-6">
The conversions here use a <span class="text-[#ff6b35] font-semibold">175cm human baseline</span> — the same one most of the hobby uses, as far as I can tell. I print the numbers before I post them. If a conversion does not look right on my table, I change it. If enough people message me saying a specific system needs different math, I update it. That is the deal.
</p>
<p class="text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
All calculations run in your browser. I never see your STL files, and nothing gets uploaded to any server.
</p>
<div class="mt-12 text-center">
<p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-500">
